# Description list

<h2 id="purpose">Purpose</h2>

A description list pairs a term with its description. It suits facts about one thing: a device and its area, a setting and its value, or a word and its meaning. Home Assistant has no card or element of this kind. A description list is plain HTML.

This page owns the markup. For rows that also carry change links or buttons, use a [summary list](summary-list.html). To compare the same values across many items, use a [Data table](table.html).

This page covers:

- Description list
  - `<dl>`
    - `<dt>`
    - `<dd>`

<h2 id="anatomy">Anatomy</h2>

A list of items. Each item is one or more terms followed by one or more descriptions. A term is a short name. A description is its value or explanation. On a wide screen the term sits beside its description. On a narrow screen it sits above.

<h2 id="variants">Variants, variables and extensibility</h2>

<h3 id="child-dl"><code>&lt;dl&gt;</code></h3>

The list itself. It holds groups of terms and descriptions, and a group may be wrapped in a `div` so each pair can be styled together. Browsers add an indent to each description by default.

No ARIA role named “description list” exists, so screen readers differ in how they read it. Some announce the number of items and some do not. Write each term so it still makes sense if read alone.

Colour comes from the theme’s text colours: `primary-text-color` for descriptions, `secondary-text-color` for terms if you want them quieter, and `divider-color` for lines between items. In light mode these are `#0B0C0C`, `#484949` and `#CECECE`. In dark mode they are `#FFFFFF`, `#CBD0D3` and `#50565A`.

Home Assistant offers no description list for a dashboard. The Entities card shows a list of entities, and the More info dialog shows an entity’s attributes. A Markdown card keeps `dl`, `dt`, `dd` and `div` tags, because its sanitiser allows them, so you can write a description list there as HTML. The sanitiser removes `style` attributes and scripts.

<h3 id="child-dl--dt"><code>&lt;dt&gt;</code></h3>

The term. Keep it short and use the same words each time: “Area”, “Last reported”. Do not put a heading, a header or a section inside a term.

<h3 id="child-dl--dd"><code>&lt;dd&gt;</code></h3>

The description. Give one idea in each. A term may have more than one description, such as two phone numbers. Put units next to the value: “19.8°C”. A missing value says “Not available”, not a blank.

<h2 id="limits">Advantages and limitations</h2>

- Pairs meaning with the term in the markup, with no table needed.
- Works in one column, so it suits a narrow screen.
- Screen reader support for the list is uneven.
- It cannot carry actions by itself. Use a summary list for that.
- Long descriptions make the term hard to find. Move a long explanation into its own paragraph.

<h2 id="usage">Usage policy and cognitive accessibility</h2>

Use a description list for a short set of facts about one thing. Keep the order the same across devices: area first, state second, last reported third.

Stack the term above its description at 320 pixels so nothing is cut off. Check that the list still reads well with larger text and wider spacing.

Success criteria that apply:

- 1.3.1 Info and Relationships: the term and its description stay linked.
- 1.4.10 Reflow and 1.4.12 Text Spacing: the list reflows and survives wider spacing.
- 2.4.6 Headings and Labels: terms describe what follows.

Short terms, one idea in each description and a steady order keep the load low.

